t yet proven.\n\n2.2. Moat assessment (0\u20133 each):\n- Intangible assets/brand: 2 (Sphere is a globally recognized icon; content IP like Wizard of Oz is licensed, not owned)\n- Switching costs: 1 (low for consumers; high for venue partners once contracts signed)\n- Network effects: 1 (limited; more valuable with more venues/content sharing)\n- Cost advantage: 2 (proprietary LED and spatial audio tech; no competitor can replicate at similar cost)\n- Economies of scale: 1 (single venue; limited purchasing/content leverage)\n- Regulatory barriers: 1 (permits/entitlements matter but are not durable)\n- Data moat: 1 (audience data exists but not monetized at scale)\n- Total: 9/21 \u2192 NARROW MOAT. Direction: STABLE-TO-NARROWING at the RSN; WIDENING at the Sphere brand (Wizard of Oz >$400M ticket sales [Company release, 2026]).\n\n2.3. Lynch classification: TURNAROUND / SPECULATIVE FAST-GROWER. Core checkpoint: whether the Las Vegas venue can self-fund content and generate positive FCF without parent capex.\n\n2.4. Financial strength scorecard:\n- Revenue: FY2023 $0.574B, FY2024 $1.027B, FY2025 $1.220B, TTM $1.357B [Actual] (note FY2023 discontinuity may involve basis change).\n- Operating margin: FY2025 \u201318.8%; 8-yr median \u201313.3% [Actual].\n- Net income: FY2025 +$0.033B [Actual]; TTM \u2013$0.070B [Actual].\n- ROE: \u20133.1% [Aggregator].\n- FCF: TTM \u2013$0.607B [Actual, if capex $1.005B is properly classified].\n- Net debt: total debt $0.961B [Aggregator] \u2013 cash $0.508B [Aggregator] = $0.453B [Estimate].\n- Interest coverage: [Unconfirmed] but weak given negative operating income.\n- Judgment: ADEQUATE-TO-WEAK. Top-line momentum is real, but FCF is negative and the balance sheet is thin.\n\n2.5. Capital allocation scorecard (0\u20133 each):\n- Buyback efficiency: 0 (no buybacks; heavy dilution potential)\n- M&A track record: 0 (MSG Networks merger has destroyed value; goodwill impairment $65.4M [TradingView summary of 10-K])\n- Capex efficiency: 2