 $400\u2013540M. To reach that, the company needs total AOI of at least $600\u2013750M after deducting SBC and normalized capex. That requires either a much larger global venue network or materially higher margins than today.\n- Judgment: REQUIRES SUCCESSFUL INTERNATIONAL EXPANSION. Probability currently estimated at <40% (below the >50% threshold the price implies).\n\n3.2. Scenario bridge (equity values, $ per share):\n\n| Scenario | Probability | Assumptions | Equity value | Per share |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Bear | 25% | Las Vegas Sphere AOI margin 15%, no international fees, MSG Networks bankrupt, one-time capex surge persists, equity raise needed | $1.5B | $40 |\n| Base | 50% | LV Sphere revenue reaches $1.2B with 25\u201328% AOI by 2030; 2\u20133 international Spheres signed by 2032 with >$100M high-margin fees; normalized capex $200M; WACC 10\u201311% | $3.6B | $100 |\n| Bull | 25% | 5+ international Spheres, content licensing across network, LV AOI >30%; FCF >$500M by 2032 | $8.0B | $220 |\n\n- Expected equity value = 0.25\u00d7$40 + 0.50\u00d7$100 + 0.25\u00d7$220 = $115/share.\n- 50% confidence interval: $70\u2013$160/share. 80% CI: $40\u2013$220/share.\n- Margin of safety = ($115 \u2013 $158.36) / $115 = \u201337.7% \u2192 NONE. The price is above the expectation.\n\n3.3. Sensitivity to key drivers:\n- If capex normalizes to $150M and WACC is 9%, the Base case rises to ~$140/share \u2192 stock roughly fairly valued.\n- If international fee revenue reaches $300M by 2032, Base case rises to ~$180/share.\n- The 3 key variables (Thesis KPIs) are: (1) international licensing revenue; (2) Sphere segment AOI margin; (3) true ongoing capex intensity.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\n- Competitive response: If Sphere's international expansion succeeds, premium venue operators (Live Nation, IMAX, MSG) will replicate or acquire similar technology; content creators (studios) will demand higher revenue shares, squeezing margins. Impact: \u20131 to \u20132.\n- Reflexivity: Rising stock price helps SPHR attract talent, use stock as M&A currency, and strengthen negotiating position with potential venue partners. Falling price would reverse this. Currently positive (price +/\u2013); but a reversal would amplify downside. Impact: +1 in bull, \u20133 in bear.\n- Customer behavior: Early-adopter novelty driven; repeat-visit and subscription/community behavior is unproven. Risk of \"immersive fatigue\" [Web Research Brief] raises the required content cadence and production spend.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\n- Prior: platform transition base rate 20\u201330%.\n- Evidence updates:\n    + Wizard of Oz >$400M ticket sales in <1 year (company release [Actual]) \u2192 raises probability of core success.\n    + Abu Dhabi construction underway, DCT-funded $1.7B (company release [Actual]) \u2192 raises probability of one international venue; but no SPHR fee economics disclosed \u2192 limited update.\n    + National Harbor third-party financing \"near term\" (company prepared remarks [Actual, but not definitive]) \u2192 raises probability of a second venue; still no signed financing agreement [Unconfirmed].\n    + Q2 2026 Sphere AOI +60% YoY (company release [Actual]) \u2192 strong core execution.\n    + MSG Networks AOI \u201370% YoY [Actual] \u2192 negative drag persists.\n- Posterior: Bear 25%, Base 50%, Bull 25%. These incorporate the positive execution evidence but are held below the market-implied probabilities by the absence of signed international license-fee agreements.\n\n\u25a0 SECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\n- The current price embeds success through 2032\u20132035 (multiple global Spheres). Our defended horizon is 3\u20135 years (to 2029\u20132031). The spread is >2 years \u2192 a potential arbitrage would be to wait for evidence (Abu Dhabi completed, National Harbor financing closed, second/third venue licensing signed). No arbitrage exists at T0 because the market is pricing the far future 