estones (2029 target).\n3. Sphere Las Vegas quarterly revenue >$300M with AOI margin >30%.\n4. Any new Sphere licensing agreement (Tokyo, London, South Korea).\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n----------------------------------------------------------------\n- Tail correlation with a 2022-style rate shock: high, because the equity is long-dated and the company needs refinancing; a high-rate environment would compress the already thin funding stack. Historical tail correlation of high-beta experiential names is ~0.7\u20130.9 in drawdowns [Estimate].\n- Conditional VaR (80% tail): if a recession hits, Sphere events revenue could fall 40\u201360%, OCF could turn negative, and with $0.44B net debt and $0.521B cash, equity value is at risk of partial or near-total impairment. Estimated long CVaR: -80%. [Assumption]\n- In the red-team scenario, SPHR could survive as a single-venue business worth $20\u201330/share, implying -80\u201385% downside from T0.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 8. MONITORING RULES\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nThesis KPIs (re-evaluate if breached):\n1. Sphere Las Vegas quarterly revenue <$200M, or AOI margin <20% for two consecutive quarters.\n2. Sphere show count or forward ticketing data (not in pack) falls >20% YoY.\n3. MSG Networks revenue decline >25% in any quarter.\n4. Cash <$300M while TTM capex >$400M.\n5. Any equity issuance >5% of shares outstanding for operating liquidity.\n6. National Harbor financing not closed by 2027-06-30.\n7. Abu Dhabi construction materially delayed past 2029 target.\n8. Any related-party transaction with MSG entities on terms unfavourable to SPHR minorities.\n\nPre-Mortem (if this fails by 2031):\n- Cause 1: No new venue financing; story collapses to a single flagship.\n- Cause 2: Vegas content fatigue; attendance fades; repeated large content write-downs.\n- Cause 3: Credit/liquidity crisis forces dilutive financing.\nEarly warnings: booking momentum, package sales, second-year attendance, and management guidance on content capex.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 9. RED TEAM (SHORT THESIS)\n----------------------------------------------------------------\nShort thesis:\n1. Current EV of $6.1B requires $1.5B of FCFF by 2035 at 12% WACC; that is roughly 8\u201310 successful Spheres, while only one exists today.\n2. SPHR is not funding the new Spheres, so its economic share is a fee slice. If a venue generates $500M revenue, a 5\u201310% license/management fee yields only $25\u201350M EBITDA. You need 20+ venues to justify this market cap.\n3. TTM FCF is -$607M; the company is dependent on capital markets at exactly the moment central banks are still cautious.\n4. MSG Networks is a declining, possibly bankrupt legacy unit; it consumes management time and may leave liabilities.\n5. Institutional ownership is 1.35%: no institutional bid 